     2|
     3|_A library that provides access to the local file system on the device._
     4|
     5|Available on platforms android, ios, tvos
     6|
     7|> **important** The `legacy` version of the FileSystem API is included in the `expo-file-system` library. It can be used alongside the modern API for backward compatibility reasons.
     8|
     9|`expo-file-system` provides access to a file system stored locally on the device. It is also capable of uploading and downloading files from network URLs.
    10|
    11|#### Diagram explaining how expo-file-system interacts with different resources
    12|
    13|[Diagram of the various pieces of expo-file-system and how they interact with different resources](https://docs.expo.dev/static/images/sdk/file-system/file-system-diagram.png)
    14|
    15|#### How expo-file-system works differently inside of the Expo Go app
    16|
    17|Within Expo Go, each project has a separate file system scope and has no access to the file system
    18|of other projects.
    19|
    20|## Installation
    21|
    22|```bash
    23|$ bunx expo install expo-file-system
    24|```
    25|
    26|If you are installing this in an existing React Native app, make sure to install `expo` in your project.
    27|
    28|## Usage
    29|
    30|### Downloading files
    31|
    32|```js Component.js
    33|const callback = (downloadProgress) => {
    34|  const progress = downloadProgress.totalBytesWritten / downloadProgress.totalBytesExpectedToWrite;
    35|  this.setState({
    36|    downloadProgress: progress,
    37|  });
    38|};
    39|
    40|const downloadResumable = FileSystem.createDownloadResumable(
    41|  "http://techslides.com/demos/sample-videos/small.mp4",
    42|  FileSystem.documentDirectory + "small.mp4",
    43|  {},
    44|  callback,
    45|);
    46|
    47|try {
    48|  const { uri } = await downloadResumable.downloadAsync();
    49|  console.log("Finished downloading to ", uri);
    50|} catch (e) {
    51|  console.error(e);
    52|}
    53|
    54|try {
    55|  await downloadResumable.pauseAsync();
    56|  console.log("Paused download operation, saving for future retrieval");
    57|  AsyncStorage.setItem("pausedDownload", JSON.stringify(downloadResumable.savable()));
    58|} catch (e) {
    59|  console.error(e);
    60|}
    61|
    62|try {
    63|  const { uri } = await downloadResumable.resumeAsync();
    64|  console.log("Finished downloading to ", uri);
    65|} catch (e) {
    66|  console.error(e);
    67|}
    68|
    69|//To resume a download across app restarts, assuming the DownloadResumable.savable() object was stored:
    70|const downloadSnapshotJson = await AsyncStorage.getItem("pausedDownload");
    71|const downloadSnapshot = JSON.parse(downloadSnapshotJson);
    72|const downloadResumable = new FileSystem.DownloadResumable(
    73|  downloadSnapshot.url,
    74|  downloadSnapshot.fileUri,
    75|  downloadSnapshot.options,
    76|  callback,
    77|  downloadSnapshot.resumeData,
    78|);
    79|
    80|try {
    81|  const { uri } = await downloadResumable.resumeAsync();
    82|  console.log("Finished downloading to ", uri);
    83|} catch (e) {
    84|  console.error(e);
    85|}
    86|```
