   146|### Server: handling multipart requests
   147|
   148|The simple server in Node.js, which can save uploaded images to disk:
   149|
   150|```js index.js
   151|const express = require("express");
   152|const app = express();
   153|const fs = require("fs");
   154|const multer = require("multer");
   155|const upload = multer({ dest: "uploads/" });
   156|
   157|// This method will save the binary content of the request as a file.
   158|app.patch("/binary-upload", (req, res) => {
   159|  req.pipe(fs.createWriteStream("./uploads/image" + Date.now() + ".png"));
   160|  res.end("OK");
   161|});
   162|
   163|// This method will save a "photo" field from the request as a file.
   164|app.patch("/multipart-upload", upload.single("photo"), (req, res) => {
   165|  // You can access other HTTP parameters. They are located in the body object.
   166|  console.log(req.body);
   167|  res.end("OK");
   168|});
   169|
   170|app.listen(3000, () => {
   171|  console.log("Working on port 3000");
   172|});
   173|```
   174|
   175|## API
   176|
   177|```js
   178|import * as FileSystem from "expo-file-system/legacy";
   179|```
   180|
   181|### Directories
   182|
   183|The API takes `file://` URIs pointing to local files on the device to identify files. Each app only has read and write access to locations under the following directories:
   184|
   185|- [`FileSystem.documentDirectory`](#filesystemdocumentdirectory)
   186|- [`FileSystem.cacheDirectory`](#filesystemcachedirectory)
   187|
   188|So, for example, the URI to a file named `'myFile'` under `'myDirectory'` in the app's user documents directory would be `FileSystem.documentDirectory + 'myDirectory/myFile'`.
   189|
   190|Expo APIs that create files generally operate within these directories. This includes `Audio` recordings, `Camera` photos, `ImagePicker` results, `SQLite` databases and `takeSnapShotAsync()` results. This allows their use with the `FileSystem` API.
   191|
   192|Some `FileSystem` functions are able to read from (but not write to) other locations.
   193|
   194|### SAF URI
   195|
   196|A SAF URI is a URI that is compatible with the Storage Access Framework. It should look like this `content://com.android.externalstorage.*`.
   197|The easiest way to obtain such URI is by [`requestDirectoryPermissionsAsync`](#requestdirectorypermissionsasyncinitialfileurl) method.
   198|
   199|## API: expo-file-system-legacy
   200|
   201|### DownloadResumable (_Class_)
   202|
   203|#### Methods
   204|
   205|- `cancelAsync(): Promise<void>`
   206|
   207|- `downloadAsync(): Promise<undefined | FileSystemDownloadResult>`
   208|  Download the contents at a remote URI to a file in the app's file system.
   209|  Returns: Returns a Promise that resolves to `FileSystemDownloadResult` object, or to `undefined` when task was cancelled.
   210|
   211|- `pauseAsync(): Promise<DownloadPauseState>`
   212|  Pause the current download operation. `resumeData` is added to the `DownloadResumable` object after a successful pause operation.
   213|  Returns an object that can be saved with `AsyncStorage` for future retrieval (the same object that is returned from calling `FileSystem.DownloadResumable.savable()`).
   214|  Returns: Returns a Promise that resolves to `DownloadPauseState` object.
   215|
   216|- `resumeAsync(): Promise<undefined | FileSystemDownloadResult>`
   217|  Resume a paused download operation.
   218|  Returns: Returns a Promise that resolves to `FileSystemDownloadResult` object, or to `undefined` when task was cancelled.
   219|
   220|- `savable(): DownloadPauseState`
   221|  Method to get the object which can be saved with `AsyncStorage` for future retrieval.

